sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/metabolic-scaling-exponent--grok-4-7--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Finite-generation networks and the approach to 3/4" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> ;
    dct:description "The Lean theorem is an infinite-generation statement. Build the finite network the hypotheses describe: branching ratio 2, generations G from 2 to 24, area-preserving branch radii, terminal units of fixed size, service volume the sum of the terminal service volumes. Define metabolic rate as total terminal flow. Regress log rate on log service volume across the generation sequence and report the local exponent between successive generations. The numerical claim matching the theorem is that the local exponent is within 0.01 of 3/4 by some G at most 24. Also build the alternative network from the critique's service-volume assumption and report its exponent. This checks the algebra of both lemmas and says nothing about real mammals." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "The primary network's local exponent lies within 0.01 of 3/4 at some generation at most 24, and the alternative network's exponent is reported from the same code." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The primary exponent stays more than 0.01 away from 3/4 through generation 24. The finite network then does not realize the theorem, and one of the two must be wrong." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/computation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Implement both finite networks from the Lean hypotheses. 2. Compute total flow and service volume at each generation from 2 to 24. 3. Estimate the local log-log exponent between successive generations. 4. Test whether the primary network is within 0.01 of 3/4 by generation 24. 5. Report the alternative network's exponent beside it." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: allometry" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-01> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-10> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.0269-8463.2004.00830.x> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Is there a universal metabolic scaling exponent?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> dct:title "A General Model for the Origin of Allometric Scaling Laws in Biology" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "West, Brown and Enquist 1997, the network being generated" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.0269-8463.2004.00830.x> dct:title "Is West, Brown and Enquist's model of allometric scaling mathematically correct and biologically relevant?" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Kozlowski and Konarzewski 2004, the alternative service-volume assumption" .
}

